I'm trying to be able to make data structures which can contain references to other data structures that implement a certain trait. A simple example being `Vector[Animal]` where `Animal` is a trait. However, it's unclear if either the type system doesn't support this yet, or I'm doing something wrong. I receive the error `Type Animal does not implement trait Default.` You can do something similar in Rust, but it requires `Box<dyn Animal>` since the structs are stack allocated vs. Moonbit structs are already heap allocated. Perhaps my Rust experience and the similarities are pushing towards patterns that aren't actually idiomatic in Moonbit?

### Moonbit

```rust
trait Animal  {
  make_sound(Self) -> String
}

struct Cat {

} derive(Default)

fn Cat::make_sound(self : Cat) -> String {
  "meow"
}

struct Dog {

} derive(Default)

fn Dog::make_sound(self : Dog) -> String {
  "woof"
}

struct Vec[T] {
  data : Array[T]
  mut length : Int
}

fn Vec::new[T : Default](size : Int) -> Vec[T] {
  { data: Array::make(size, T::default()), length: 0 }
}

fn push[T](self : Vec[T], value : T) -> Unit {
  self.data[self.length] = value
  self.length = self.length + 1
}

fn init {
  let animals : Vec[Animal] = Vec::new(2) // Type Animal does not implement trait Default.
  animals.push(Cat::{} as Animal)
  animals.push(Dog::{} as Animal)
}
```

<img width="588" alt="image" src="https://github.com/moonbitlang/moonbit-docs/assets/762949/b355a06b-66ff-45bf-8373-2d4cdf4a55d5">


### Equivalent Rust

```rust
trait Animal {
    fn make_sound(&self) -> String;
}

#[derive(Default)]
struct Cat {
    // cat specific state
}

impl Animal for Cat {
    fn make_sound(&self) -> String {
        "meow".to_string()
    }
}

#[derive(Default)]
struct Dog {
    // dog specific state
}

impl Animal for Dog {
    fn make_sound(&self) -> String {
        "woof".to_string()
    }
}

fn main() {
    let mut animals: Vec<Box<dyn Animal>> = Vec::new();
    animals.push(Box::new(Cat {}));
    animals.push(Box::new(Dog {}));
}
```

